{"name":"Nectar of the Goddess","hex":"#e3a701","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"nectar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The drink of the gods. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Any delicious drink, now especially a type of sweetened fruit juice."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To feed on nectar."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nectar"},{"term":"goddess","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A female deity.","An incredibly beautiful, gorgeous, sexy, attractive woman honored and adored for her beauty or of superior charm and intelligence."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/goddess"}],"entries":[{"title":"Nectar","blocks":[{"text":"Nectar is a viscous, sugar-rich liquid produced by plants in glands called nectaries, either within the flowers with which it attracts pollinating animals, or by extrafloral nectaries, which provide a nutrient source to animal mutualists, which in turn provide herbivore protection. Common nectar-consuming pollinators include mosquitoes, hoverflies, wasps, bees, butterflies and moths, hummingbirds, honeyeaters and bats. \nNectar is an economically important substance as it is the sugar source for honey."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Nectar.jpg/330px-Nectar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nectar","wikidata":"Q171934"},{"title":"Goddess","blocks":[{"text":"A goddess is a female deity. In some faiths, a sacred female figure holds a central place in religious prayer and worship. For example, Shaktism, holds that the ultimate deity, the source of all reality, is Supreme Goddess (Mahamaya) and in some forms of Tantric Shaivism, the pair of Shiva and Shakti are the ultimate principle. Meanwhile, in Vajrayana Buddhism, ultimate reality is often seen as being composed of two principles depicted as two deities in union symbolising the non-duality of the two principles of perfect wisdom (female) and skillful compassion (male). A single figure in a monotheistic faith that is female may be identified simply as god because of no need to differentiate by gender or with a diminutive. An experiment to determine the effect of psychedelics on subjects composed of leaders from diverse religious groups revealed a general experience that the divine which the subjects encountered was feminine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Queen_Nefertari_being_led_by_Isis_MET_DP167142.jpg/330px-Queen_Nefertari_being_led_by_Isis_MET_DP167142.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goddess","wikidata":"Q205985"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg/500px-Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg/1280px-Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg/500px-Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Orange-yellow nectaries and greenish nectar in buckwheat flowers","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABuckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Australian_painted_lady_feeding_closeup.jpg/500px-Australian_painted_lady_feeding_closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Australian_painted_lady_feeding_closeup.jpg/500px-Australian_painted_lady_feeding_closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An Australian painted lady feeding on a flower's nectar","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAustralian_painted_lady_feeding_closeup.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/0rchideen_04.jpg/500px-0rchideen_04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/0rchideen_04.jpg/1280px-0rchideen_04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/0rchideen_04.jpg/500px-0rchideen_04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gymnadenia conopsea flowers with nectar-filled spur","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A0rchideen_04.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Extrafloral_nectaries_of_a_Wild_Cherry.jpg/500px-Extrafloral_nectaries_of_a_Wild_Cherry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Extrafloral_nectaries_of_a_Wild_Cherry.jpg/1280px-Extrafloral_nectaries_of_a_Wild_Cherry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Extrafloral_nectaries_of_a_Wild_Cherry.jpg/500px-Extrafloral_nectaries_of_a_Wild_Cherry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AExtrafloral_nectaries_of_a_Wild_Cherry.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Prunus_africana_nectaries.jpg/500px-Prunus_africana_nectaries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Prunus_africana_nectaries.jpg/1280px-Prunus_africana_nectaries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb